import{_ as t,o as r,c as o,a0 as s}from"./chunks/framework.D4fUSYzX.js";const m=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR 0161: The herdr-core derivation bounds the session record by the host's terminal creation time","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0161-the-herdr-core-derivation-bounds-the-session-record-by-the-hosts-terminal-creation-time.md","filePath":"adr/0161-the-herdr-core-derivation-bounds-the-session-record-by-the-hosts-terminal-creation-time.md"}`),n={name:"adr/0161-the-herdr-core-derivation-bounds-the-session-record-by-the-hosts-terminal-creation-time.md"};function a(h,e,i,d,c,l){return r(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="adr-0161-the-herdr-core-derivation-bounds-the-session-record-by-the-host-s-terminal-creation-time" tabindex="-1">ADR 0161: The herdr-core derivation bounds the session record by the host&#39;s terminal creation time <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0161-the-herdr-core-derivation-bounds-the-session-record-by-the-host-s-terminal-creation-time" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ADR 0161: The herdr-core derivation bounds the session record by the host&#39;s terminal creation time&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Status: accepted Date: 2026-11-14</p><p>Records that ADR 0144&#39;s identity facts gain a second bound: when the herdr-core host reports the time it created the run&#39;s terminal, that time stands in for the start time the plane recorded as the earliest time the record can exist. The recorded start keeps the bound when the host reports no creation time, and the reader&#39;s staleness guard stays on the recorded start. It changes no wording the operator reads and it lands no new supplier; it amends the identity facts of <a href="./0144-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-derives-the-agent-session-record-path.html">ADR 0144</a> with a fact the host already reports.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>ADR 0144 picks the record by the Agent type, the checkout the Agent runs in, and the start time the plane already recorded: a record that began before the recorded start cannot be the run&#39;s. The plane records the start when the host confirms it: the plane asks the host to start the run, and only when that answer comes back does the handoff&#39;s start time exist. The runtime opens its session record when it boots, inside that answer&#39;s round trip, before the plane writes the start time.</p><p>So on the herdr-core host the record the runtime opens for the run begins before the start the plane records for it. Measured on 2026-11-14 against <code>hc</code> 0.1.x and pi 1.5.2 on this machine (<a href="https://github.com/SeriousJul/my-little-software-factory/blob/main/docs/verification/agent-host-herdr-core.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the verification record</a>): the host&#39;s terminal record says the terminal was created at 12:58:37.509Z, the pi record file it booted stands stamped 12:58:37.660Z, and the plane recorded the run&#39;s start at 12:58:39.722Z. A bound of &quot;the record began at or after the recorded start&quot; rejects the run&#39;s own record, the derivation answers unavailable, the settle takes the terminal capture with the fail-open <code>unknown</code> cause of ADR 0017, the completed turn records no workflow transition, and a settled turn on an operator-decides position offers no merge row. The race is systematic on this host: the host always confirms the start after the record exists.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>The host&#39;s own time stands in for the plane&#39;s.</strong> The herdr-core terminal record&#39;s creation time is the earliest time the record can exist, and it bounds both the record&#39;s file stamp and the record&#39;s own start time in its header. The order is strict on the host&#39;s own facts: the plane claims the run, the host starts it, the host creates the terminal, the runtime boots, the runtime opens the record, the turn ends, and the plane settles. No tolerance is needed: the record is opened after the terminal exists, on the host&#39;s own clock.</p><p><strong>The recorded start keeps its bound when the host reports none.</strong> A host without a creation time, or an old host record without the field, gives the derivation no earlier bound: the recorded start of ADR 0144 stands, and the derivation&#39;s answer for that run is exactly what it is today. The fix changes nothing for a host that carries no fact.</p><p><strong>The reader&#39;s staleness guard stays on the recorded start.</strong> The per-kind reader still rejects a turn end earlier than the start the plane recorded, because a turn end is always after the recorded start: the guard tests a turn&#39;s time, not the record&#39;s identity. Only the identity&#39;s bound moves.</p><h2 id="considered-options" tabindex="-1">Considered options <a class="header-anchor" href="#considered-options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Considered options&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A fixed tolerance on ADR 0144&#39;s bound.</strong> Rejected: a tolerance guesses the host&#39;s round trip, and a host whose round trip runs longer than the tolerance admits a record the run did not open. The host&#39;s own creation time needs no guess.</li><li><strong>The newest record in the directory.</strong> Rejected: ADR 0144 is a lookup in the open, never a guess, and newest is a rank, not an identity: it accepts a stale record when the runtime reopens one, and it breaks on a second terminal in the same checkout.</li><li><strong>Record the start before the host&#39;s start call.</strong> Rejected: the plane&#39;s pre-call time is the plane&#39;s fact, and the plane&#39;s clock can stand before the host&#39;s. The host&#39;s creation time is the host&#39;s own statement of when the run began on the host, and the plane already reads the record that carries it.</li><li><strong>Ask herdr-core to report the session record.</strong> Still rejected, for the reasons ADR 0144 gave. This decision is the same lookup on one more fact the host already reports, and it keeps the ask open.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>ADR 0144&#39;s identity facts are now the Agent type, the checkout, and a bound: the host&#39;s terminal creation time when the host reports one, the recorded start when it does not. The verification record names the bound and the measurement that found the race.</li><li>A host that reports its creation time late, or in another unit, changes the bound&#39;s meaning. The read is one number in the host&#39;s record, and a host without the number degrades to ADR 0144&#39;s bound: today&#39;s behavior, one line in the run&#39;s record, and the settle&#39;s answer is unchanged.</li><li>The settle of a herdr-core-launched Agent now reads the Agent&#39;s own record in the case the measurement found. The flow ADR 0144 recorded as unmeasured is measured on this machine (2026-11-14, <code>hc</code> 0.1.x, pi 1.5.2), and the other hosts and runtimes stay unmeasured, as the record states.</li></ul>',14)])])}const p=t(n,[["render",a]]);export{m as __pageData,p as default};
